      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d2ce04f1-504c-5fae-b7a9-b70a50ece8ab",
        ":question/problem": "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,-4)$ and $(-4,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(0,-4)$ and $(-4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-4)}{-4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+4}{-4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{-4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (- \\frac{7}{4})=-1\\,\u21d2\\,m=\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-0) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{4 x}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4 x}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "a227f9d9-d862-5ed6-a5bf-08a165ad84fc",
