        ":question/worked-solution": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(0,-4)$ and $(-4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-4)}{-4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{-4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (- \\frac{7}{4})=-1\\,\u21d2\\,m=\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(1,-6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-1) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-1) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{4 x}{7} - \\frac{4}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4 x}{7} - \\frac{46}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
